  "notes": "British house-pop vocalist with a gospel-soul delivery, and the entry is explicit that the singer is why the harmony is richer than the lane's norm: \"gospel plagal cadences and 7th/9th extensions — the harmony is richer than the lane's norm because the topline is written for a real soul singer\". So `extensions` runs 0.32/0.44/0.24 and the families carry the plagal iv-i motion. The topline is the other half — \"wide-interval, melisma-heavy and belted at the top of the chest voice — the parameter that distinguishes the 2010s British house-pop wave from Eurodance's thin hook singers\" — which is the widest leap share among the vocal-fronted models here and a high octave-jump range. The kit is \"house kick on all four, clap on 2&4, offbeat open hats, with a lightly swung 16th hat carried from garage\", so `swing.amount` sits at 0.55, and the Clean Bandit records \"put string stabs on the backbeat\". The bass is \"deep-house-style syncopated synth bass with a walking motion between chord tones\", an independent riff at 0.5 roots. ⚠ The research marks the harmony `[LOW-CONF]` and calls the parameters lane-derived; both marks are repeated here, and 0.55, the velocities and every density are INFERRED.",
